{
  // EVERY `.mjs` THIS REPOSITORY RUNS AS AN INSTRUMENT, read with the same lens as everything
  // else. `plugin/hooks/session-start.mjs` is SHIPPED — it is the first thing a Claude Code user
  // receives from this product — and the five under `.github/` decide whether a red was the guard
  // or the machine, how often the suite lied, what it left in `/tmp`, and whether the change
  // about to be merged credits the tool that wrote it. An error in a ruler is an error in the
  // ruler, and this bench has measured what a broken ruler costs twice.
  //
  // THE COUNT IS NOT WRITTEN HERE, and that is the second thing this comment has learnt: it said
  // FIVE until 2026-09-12 and a sixth instrument arrived without it. The globs below reach the
  // tree, and `the-rulers-are-read-by-the-rules.test.ts` reconciles what they reach against the
  // disk in both directions.
  //
  // WHY A PROJECT AND NOT A SCRIPT. `pnpm typecheck` is `tsc -b` over the references below, and
  // a second entry point would be a second answer to "is this repository type-checked". This is
  // the FIFTH reference of `tsconfig.json`, so the command that already exists reaches them.
  //
  // `noEmit`, because nothing consumes a declaration of these: the host spawns the hook by path
  // with no build, and the rulers are run by `node` from the repository. Everything else comes
  // from `tsconfig.base.json`, which is the point — the same `strict`, the same
  // `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, the same `exactOptionalPropertyTypes` the product is held to.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "rootDir": ".",
    "types": ["node"]
  },
  "include": [".github/**/*.mjs", "plugin/**/*.mjs"]
}
